 To find the modular inverse of \(11 \mod 1000\), we need to find an integer \(x\) such that \(11x \equiv 1 \pmod{1000}\). This is equivalent to finding \(x\) such that \(11x = 1000k + 1\) for some integer \(k\). This can be solved using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, which finds solutions to equations of the form \(ax + by = \gcd(a, b)\). Since \(11\) and \(1000\) are coprime, we know there is a solution. The steps of the Extended Euclidean Algorithm are as follows: \[
\begin{aligned}
1000 &= 11 \cdot 90 + 10, \\
11 &= 10 \cdot 1 + 1, \\
10 &= 1 \cdot 10 + 0.
\end{aligned}
\] Reversing the steps to express \(1\) as a linear combination of \(11\) and \(1000\), we get: \[
\begin{aligned}
1 &= 11 - 10 \cdot 1, \\
1 &= 11 - (1000 - 11 \cdot 90) = 11 \cdot 91 - 1000.
\end{aligned}
\] Thus, \(11 \cdot 91 - 1000 \cdot 1 = 1\), so the modular inverse of \(11 \mod 1000\) is \(91\). </think> <answer> 91 </answer>

